  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1041 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nLatest scans from KNQA do show some showers ongoing near the TN\nRiver, with additional showers and thunderstorms to our\nnorthwest. The cluster of storms to our west / northwest will\ncontinue to move towards our area along a decaying MCV through\nthe overnight hours. So far, storms have remained relatively tame\nover the past few hours with only a few special weather\nstatements to note in surrounding areas. Taking a look at our\ncurrent environment, storms will likely struggle even further as\nthey reach our area. There is currently a fairly decent cap in\nplace, with low and mid-level lapse rates currently < 6 C/km.\nThere is still >1,500 j/kg of MUCAPE if storms can find a way to\ntap into the potential, but much of the latest guidance does not\nsuggest that this will be the case. Nonetheless, the forecast\nwill carry scattered thunderstorm chances across the far\nnorthwest portions of the CWA with isolated chances elsewhere\ninto the early morning hours.\n\nInto Saturday, CAMs continue to struggle with the placement and\nevolution of the aforementioned MCV, which will still be\ntraversing the area through the daytime. The best chances for any\nredevelopment would likely be in the afternoon hours with peak\ndiurnal heating, but will not entirely rule out some convective\ndevelopment in the morning with the lingering boundaries. As far\nas temperatures go, much of the Mid-South will remain in the low\n90s once again. Heat index values may approach 105 for some\nlocations, mainly across the delta regions. However, with some\nCAMs developing convection across this area in the morning, this\nwill  create uncertainty. Latest HREF guidance keeps chances of\nexceeding 105 less than 20%, so opted against issuing any heat\nproducts.\n\nBy Sunday into the beginning of next week, zonal flow aloft will\ncontinue to develop with a strengthening high pressure to the\neast. This will allow for a more typical, summertime airmass to\nremain in place. With this airmass and little forcing aloft,\nthere will be daily, diurnally-driven thunderstorm chances.\nCoverage, however, will likely remain fairly isolated and more in\nthe pop-up variety rather than any organized storm system. As far\nas high temperatures go, highs will generally remain in the low\nto mid 90s, with heat index values approaching or exceeding\n105 degrees for many locations by Monday through Wednesday. Daily\nheat advisories for at least some portions of the Mid-South will\nlikely be needed through this timeframe. By the middle to end of\nnext week, an upper-level trough will begin to dig through the\nmidwest, allowing the Mid-South to return to a more northwesterly\nflow regime. This would allow for a small uptick in shower and\nthunderstorm chances, with high temperatures falling a few\ndegrees back down to near normal. There is still some uncertainty\nwith the placement and timing of this trough, which will\nultimately play a role in how next week ends.",
